WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.815
This video follows the boot chain from the chip's first instruction to Linux init, and shows how secure boot checks every handoff.

00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:14.448
init / .rc files: The first user program Android starts; its .rc files say which services start and when. Like: The stage manager with a script that says who walks on stage and when.

00:00:14.698 --> 00:00:23.626
Secure boot / chain of trust: Each boot stage checks the digital signature of the next before running it. Like: Each guard checks the next guard's badge before handing over the keys.

00:00:24.726 --> 00:00:29.800
Press the power button on the bar. About fifteen seconds later the screen shows the meeting room.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:35.088
In between, five different programs run, each checking the next one's signature.

00:00:35.338 --> 00:00:38.380
If any check fails, the bar refuses to boot.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:43.599
Only when every check passes does the first picture appear. Let's walk the chain.

00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:46.147
Booting is a relay race.

00:00:46.547 --> 00:00:52.294
Each runner is one boot stage, and passing the baton means loading and jumping to the next stage.

00:00:52.694 --> 00:00:59.207
In secure boot, every runner checks the next runner's ID badge before handing over. That's a signature check.

00:00:59.607 --> 00:01:04.959
And the list of valid badges is burned into the track itself, in one time programmable fuses.

00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:06.856
Words.

00:01:07.256 --> 00:01:12.388
The boot ROM is code fixed inside the chip when it's manufactured. It can never change.

00:01:12.788 --> 00:01:18.720
Boot ROM: Code burned into the chip at the factory that runs first and checks the next stage. Like: The building's foundation stone: cannot be changed and everything rests on it.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:26.091
The S P L, or on Qualcomm the X B L, is a small first stage loader. Its main job is setting up DRAM.

00:01:26.491 --> 00:01:30.891
U-Boot / ABL / XBL: Common bootloaders: U-Boot on many boards; ABL and XBL on Qualcomm chips. Like: Different brands of stagehand, each with its own checklist.

00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:35.707
U-Boot, or Qualcomm's A B L, is the full bootloader that loads the kernel.

00:01:36.107 --> 00:01:42.725
Bootloader: The small program that runs first at power-on, sets up memory and loads the kernel. Like: The stagehand who sets up the stage before the actors arrive.

00:01:42.975 --> 00:01:49.697
Kernel: The core of the operating system that controls memory, devices and which program runs. Like: The building manager who owns every key and decides who uses which room.

00:01:49.947 --> 00:01:55.752
T F A is ARM's trusted firmware, and the T E E is a secure world that keeps keys.

00:01:56.152 --> 00:02:02.828
Firmware: Software that runs close to hardware, usually stored in flash memory on the device. Like: The instincts built into an animal: low-level behaviour that is there from birth.

00:02:03.078 --> 00:02:08.291
TEE / TF-A: Trusted execution environment: a walled-off secure world on the processor for keys and secrets. Like: A bank vault inside the building that even the building manager cannot enter.

00:02:08.591 --> 00:02:14.872
A V B is Android verified boot. It checks Android partitions using a signed table called v b meta.

00:02:15.272 --> 00:02:18.778
AVB / dm-verity: Android Verified Boot and dm-verity: checks that the system partitions have not been changed, block by block. Like: A tamper seal on every page of the rulebook.

00:02:19.878 --> 00:02:21.201
Let's run the race.

00:02:21.601 --> 00:02:30.158
The boot ROM reads fuses to find the boot device, loads the first stage, and checks its signature against the key hash burned in fuses.

00:02:30.558 --> 00:02:37.280
The first stage sets up clocks, the power chip, and trains DRAM. Then it verifies the next images.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:43.543
Trusted firmware starts the secure monitor, and optionally a trusted OS that holds keys.

00:02:43.943 --> 00:02:51.768
U-Boot or A B L reads its environment, picks the A or B slot, loads the kernel and device tree, and verifies them.

00:02:52.168 --> 00:03:04.997
Device tree: A text map of the hardware on a board (which chips, on which pins and addresses) that the kernel reads at boot. Like: The floor plan of a house, so the electrician knows where every socket is without digging into walls.

00:03:05.297 --> 00:03:09.454
The kernel starts, prints to an early console, and probes drivers.

00:03:09.854 --> 00:03:14.474
Driver: Kernel code that knows how to operate one kind of hardware. Like: A translator who speaks one specific machine's language.

00:03:14.724 --> 00:03:19.763
Init starts: systemd or busybox on Linux, Android's init on Android.

00:03:20.163 --> 00:03:27.895
And on Android, d m verity keeps checking every block of the system partition as it's read, so tampering is caught even after boot.

00:03:29.095 --> 00:03:31.673
A few U-Boot commands worth knowing by heart.

00:03:32.073 --> 00:03:33.640
Print the kernel command line.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.719
Set it, adding earlycon so the kernel prints before its real console driver loads.

00:03:39.119 --> 00:03:42.080
Select the eMMC and list its partitions.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.488
Load the kernel image into memory.

00:03:44.888 --> 00:03:46.374
Load the device tree.

00:03:46.774 --> 00:03:51.523
Inspect the device tree right from the bootloader, for example to check an I two C node.

00:03:51.923 --> 00:04:03.277
I2C: A simple two-wire bus that lets a processor talk to small chips like sensors and HDMI bridges. Like: A classroom where the teacher calls each student by number and they answer one at a time.

00:04:03.577 --> 00:04:06.921
And boot it. The dash means no separate initramfs.

00:04:08.121 --> 00:04:10.919
When boot goes silent, narrow it down.

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:16.404
No output at all? Check UART wiring and baud rate, and the boot mode pins.

00:04:16.804 --> 00:04:24.618
UART: A simple serial port, often the first console you see when a board boots. Like: A tin-can telephone straight into the board.

00:04:24.918 --> 00:04:32.093
If you see starting kernel and then nothing, it's usually a wrong device tree, a wrong load address, or the console not enabled.

00:04:32.493 --> 00:04:37.276
Add earlycon, keep boot con, and initcall debug to see how far the kernel gets.

00:04:37.676 --> 00:04:42.529
If it hangs before any console, attach JTAG and read the program counter.

00:04:42.929 --> 00:04:51.822
JTAG / SWD: Hardware debug ports that let a debugger stop the processor and read its memory. Like: A doctor's stethoscope plugged straight into the chip.

00:04:52.122 --> 00:04:58.833
And for security: never ship debug keys, and do signing in a controlled CI step, never on a laptop.

00:04:59.233 --> 00:05:11.295
CI / HIL: Continuous integration runs tests on every change; hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) runs them on real boards. Like: A quality inspector at every station of the assembly line, some with real parts on the bench.

00:05:12.395 --> 00:05:18.897
Question for you: If an attacker replaces the kernel on the flash, what stops it from running with secure boot?

00:05:24.197 --> 00:05:35.017
Answer: The bootloader checks the kernel's signature against a trusted key before running it, and the bootloader itself was checked by the stage before. The fake kernel fails the check.

00:05:36.117 --> 00:05:36.814
Recap.

00:05:37.214 --> 00:05:42.880
The chain is ROM, first stage, trusted firmware, bootloader, kernel, then init.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:46.809
Each stage verifies the next, anchored in fuses.

00:05:47.209 --> 00:05:51.075
Anti rollback counters stop old but validly signed images.

00:05:51.475 --> 00:05:53.658
Earlycon is your first debugging tool.

00:05:54.058 --> 00:05:58.261
And lab nine simulates image verification and automatic A B fallback.
